### Article 3

Definitions

1.	The expressions used in this law have the following meanings:

1.1.	"Family Relationship" - relationship between persons who are or have been:

1.1.1.	engaged;  

1.1.2.	married;  

LAW NO. 08/L-185 ON THE PREVENTION AND PROTECTION FROM DOMESTIC VIOLENCE VIOLENCE AGAINST WOMEN AND GENDER-BASED VIOLENCE

1.1.3.	In extramarital cohabitation;  

1.1.4.	In a common household;  

1.1.5.	Are common parents of one or more children;  

1.1.6.	Use a common home and are related by blood, affinity, adoption, or are in a guardianship relationship, including parents, grandparents, children, nieces and nephews from daughters and sons, brothers and sisters, aunts, uncles, nieces and nephews from brothers and sisters, cousins.  

1.2.	"Violence against women" - is understood as a violation of human rights and a form of discrimination against women and shall mean all acts of gender-based violence that result in or are likely to result in physical, sexual, psychological or economic harm or suffering to women, including threats of such acts, coercion or arbitrary deprivation of liberty, whether in public or private life;  

1.3.	"Domestic violence" - means all acts of physical, sexual, psychological and economic violence that occur within the family relationship, or household unit, regardless of blood ties or family relationship, or by a former intimate partner or current intimate partner, regardless of the fact whether the perpetrator of violence shares or has shared the same residence with the victim with the aim of violating the dignity and maintaining under continuous control and dependent position, the persons against whom violence has been exercised;  

1.4.	"Gender-based violence against women" - means violence that is directed against a woman because she is a woman or that affects women disproportionately. Gender-based violence against women includes the causes and result of the unequal power relationship between women and men as a result of a social problem and not an individual one;  

1.5.	"Political harassment" - political harassment is one or more acts of pressure, persecution, harassment or threats, committed by a person or group of people, directly or through third parties, against women candidates, elected, appointed or in exercise of political-public function or against their families, with the aim of curtailing, suspending, preventing or limiting the essential functions in their position, to incite or force them to perform, against their will, an action or not to act, in the performance of duties or in the exercise of their rights;  

1.6.	"Political Violence" - political violence means actions, conduct and/or physical, psychological, sexual aggressions committed by a person or group of people, directly or through third parties, against women candidates, elected, appointed or in exercise of political-public function; or against her family, to curtail, suspend, prevent or limit the exercise of her duty or to incite or force her to perform, against her will, an action or not to act, in the performance of duties or in the exercise of their rights;  

1.7.	"Gender" - means the socially constructed roles, behaviors, activities and attributes that a given society considers appropriate for women and men;  

1.8.	"Psychological violence" - includes continuous control, verbal abuse, isolation, humiliation or emotional abuse through insults, offenses, blackmail, intimidation, stalking and continuous surveillance, concealment of information, misinformation and threatening behavior, degrading treatment, threats or similar forms of control or psychological pressure, placing a person under reasonable fear of the use of violence against him or persons in family relationship, threat to damage property or keeping under continuous emotional distress;  

LAW NO. 08/L-185 ON THE PREVENTION AND PROTECTION FROM DOMESTIC VIOLENCE VIOLENCE AGAINST WOMEN AND GENDER-BASED VIOLENCE

1.9.	"Economic violence" - includes denial of access to or control of basic resources through restriction of essential means for life, denial of access to medical services, education or employment, alienation of common property before/during marriage or cohabitation or after its termination, or other forms of pressure with the aim of keeping a person within a family relationship in a position of economic dependence. Economic violence may be expressed as a single form of pressure or combined with other forms of abuse or violence;  

1.10.	"Continuous control" - means a series of actions aimed at keeping a person under continuous control in a dependent and subordinate position through various forms of violence or threat of violence, exploiting their resources and capacities for personal gain, depriving them of the means necessary for independence, resistance and departure and regulating their daily behavior;  

1.11.	"Sexual harassment through the internet" - is an unwanted verbal, non-verbal action or other action of a sexual nature, which has a purpose or effect, violation of dignity or creation of a threatening, hostile, humiliating or intimidating environment, approach or practice;  

1.12.	"Battered person syndrome" - psychological condition that may develop when a person experiences abuse, usually by an intimate partner. It includes, but is not limited to - spouses, individuals who are living together, sexual partners, and individuals who do not have sexual relations. The relationships from which battered woman syndrome derives may be heterosexual or homosexual;  

1.13.	"Intimate partner" - person with whom another person is or has been in a close personal relationship, similar to a couple relationship, which may be characterized by emotional connection, continuous physical or sexual contact and who are not or have not been engaged, married or in extramarital cohabitation;  

1.14.	"Responsible party" - the person against whom any of the forms of protection specified in this law is requested, or a protection order, emergency protection order or temporary emergency protection order has been issued;  

1.15.	"Victim" - the person against whom any type of violence included in this law has been exercised;  

1.16.	"Vulnerable victim" - girls and women, children, minors, LGBTQ +, persons with limited physical and mental abilities, pregnant woman, elderly person and the person whose family relationship or dependence on the responsible party makes him/her particularly sensitive to repeated victimization, intimidation or revenge;  

1.17.	"Non-refoulement" - is a principle on the basis of which the state is obliged not to expel or return women and children asylum seekers, persons with refugee status or persons under subsidiary protection in any way to the borders of a state in which there is a direct risk to life and body, from violence against women or domestic violence, or their freedom would be endangered because of race, religion, nationality, membership in a particular social group or political belief or when they would be subjected to torture, inhuman or degrading treatment or punishment;  

1.18.	"Protected party" - the person in whose benefit the protection order has been issued;  

1.19.	"Survivor" - the person who has overcome domestic violence or violence directed against women;  

1.20.	"Authorized representative" - the person who is authorized to protect the interests

LAW NO. 08/L-185 ON THE PREVENTION AND PROTECTION FROM DOMESTIC VIOLENCE VIOLENCE AGAINST WOMEN AND GENDER-BASED VIOLENCE

of the parties in the procedure;  

1.21.	"Victim advocate" - Authorized representative for the protection of the interests of the victim and the realization of his/her rights in accordance with this law and other laws in force;  

1.22.	"Protection order" - order issued by court decision or by the police, which provides protective measures for the victim and includes the regular protection order, emergency protection order and temporary emergency protection order.

